('127.251.d.10.C',3,'A $5,000 early withdrawal from a traditional retirement account with a 10% penalty costs how much in penalty?','["$5,000", "$50", "$500", "$250"]'::jsonb,2,'0.10 x 5,000 = 500.','original'),
('127.251.d.10.C',4,'In addition to the penalty, an early withdrawal from a traditional account is usually:','["Doubled", "Tax-free", "Refunded", "Taxed as income"]'::jsonb,3,'Withdrawals count as income.','original'),
('127.251.d.10.D',3,'If a worker contributes 6% of a $50,000 salary and the employer matches 50%, the employer adds:','["$1,500", "$750", "$150", "$3,000"]'::jsonb,0,'6% of 50,000 = 3,000; half is 1,500.','original'),
('127.251.d.10.D',4,'Saving $200 a month for 30 years without interest totals:','["$7,200", "$72,000", "$24,000", "$6,000"]'::jsonb,1,'200 x 360 = 72,000.','original'),
('127.251.d.14.A',3,'A balanced budget means:','["No savings", "No bills", "Income covers expenses and savings", "Spending exceeds income"]'::jsonb,2,'Income and outflow match.','original'),
('127.251.d.14.A',4,'Using a 50/30/20 plan on $3,000 monthly income, savings should be about:','["$900", "$300", "$1,500", "$600"]'::jsonb,3,'20% of 3,000 = 600.','original'),
('127.251.d.14.B',3,'A free resource to help manage a budget is:','["A free budgeting app or spreadsheet template", "A credit card", "A high-fee service", "A loan"]'::jsonb,0,'Free tools exist.','original'),
('127.251.d.14.B',4,'Nonprofit credit counseling agencies offer:','["Free cars", "Low-cost help with budgets and debt", "High fees", "Guaranteed raises"]'::jsonb,1,'Counselors assist consumers.','original'),
('127.251.d.14.C',3,'The cost of living for the same lifestyle is generally higher in:','["Rural areas always", "Every place equally", "Large expensive cities", "Small towns always"]'::jsonb,2,'Housing drives differences.','original'),
('127.251.d.14.C',4,'A salary of $60,000 buys less in a city where housing costs:','["Are free", "Are unchanged", "Are much lower", "Are much higher"]'::jsonb,3,'Real purchasing power varies.','original'),
('127.251.d.15.A',3,'Using someone else''s Social Security number to open an account is:','["Identity theft", "A budgeting tool", "A tax credit", "A bank service"]'::jsonb,0,'It is a crime.','original'),
('127.251.d.15.A',4,'A phishing email that tricks you into sharing your password is a form of:','["A job offer", "Identity theft attempt", "A coupon", "A receipt"]'::jsonb,1,'Phishing steals information.','original'),
('127.251.d.15.B',3,'A strong prevention step is to:','["Reuse one password", "Share PINs", "Use unique passwords and two-factor authentication", "Post your SSN"]'::jsonb,2,'Strong authentication protects accounts.','original'),
('127.251.d.15.B',4,'To prevent identity theft, you should:','["Throw them in the trash", "Mail them to strangers", "Leave them on a desk", "Shred documents with personal information"]'::jsonb,3,'Shredding prevents dumpster diving.','original'),
('127.251.d.15.C',3,'A victim of identity theft should report it to the:','["Federal Trade Commission at IdentityTheft.gov", "Radio station", "School bus driver", "Pizza shop"]'::jsonb,0,'The FTC handles reports.','original'),
('127.251.d.15.C',4,'After discovering identity theft, a victim should also:','["Close all accounts silently", "Place a fraud alert or freeze on credit reports", "Ignore it", "Share the SSN publicly"]'::jsonb,1,'Alerts limit new fraud.','original'),
('127.251.d.16.A',3,'A student exploring careers should consider education, military service, and:','["Rumors", "Past events", "Current job opportunities", "Only hobbies"]'::jsonb,2,'Options include many paths.','original'),
('127.251.d.16.A',4,'The military can help pay for education through:','["Tax refunds", "Free cars", "Bank fees", "Programs such as the GI Bill"]'::jsonb,3,'Benefits support education.','original'),
('127.251.d.16.B',3,'Matching interests and skills to careers helps a student:','["Choose a suitable path and plan for it", "Delay forever", "Avoid planning", "Ignore costs"]'::jsonb,0,'Alignment improves satisfaction.','original'),
('127.251.d.16.B',4,'A student who enjoys helping people and science might research careers in:','["Only sales", "Healthcare", "Only mining", "Only forestry"]'::jsonb,1,'Interests suggest fields.','original'),
('127.251.d.16.C',3,'A program costs $12,000 per year in tuition and $8,000 in living costs for 4 years. Total cost is:','["$48,000", "$96,000", "$80,000", "$20,000"]'::jsonb,2,'(12,000 + 8,000) x 4 = 80,000.','original'),
('127.251.d.16.C',4,'To estimate total funding for college, a student should include tuition, books, housing, and:','["Only tuition", "Nothing else", "Only books", "Transportation and fees"]'::jsonb,3,'Costs go beyond tuition.','original'),
('127.251.d.16.D',3,'Aid that does not need to be repaid includes:','["Grants and scholarships", "Student loans", "Payday loans", "Credit cards"]'::jsonb,0,'Gift aid is free money.','original'),
('127.251.d.16.D',4,'The form used to apply for federal student aid is the:','["1099", "FAFSA", "W-4", "W-2"]'::jsonb,1,'The FAFSA determines federal aid.','original'),
('127.251.d.16.E',3,'Earning dual credit in high school can save money by:','["Lengthening college", "Raising tuition", "Reducing the number of college courses to pay for later", "Adding debt"]'::jsonb,2,'Early credits lower costs.','original'),
('127.251.d.16.E',4,'A student earning 12 college credits in high school at $300 per credit avoids about:','["$1,200", "$36,000", "$360", "$3,600 in tuition"]'::jsonb,3,'12 x 300 = 3,600.','original'),
('127.251.d.16.F',3,'A graph comparing the cost of a degree with expected earnings helps a student:','["Judge whether the education is a good investment", "Avoid math", "Pick a hobby", "Ignore costs"]'::jsonb,0,'Graphs show payback.','original'),
('127.251.d.16.F',4,'A career pays $30,000 more per year with a degree. A graph of cumulative extra earnings over 10 years reaches:','["$60,000", "$300,000", "$3,000,000", "$30,000"]'::jsonb,1,'30,000 x 10 = 300,000.','original'),
('127.251.d.16.G',3,'A degree costs $40,000 and adds $100,000 in earnings over time. Net gain is:','["$100,000", "$40,000", "$60,000", "$140,000"]'::jsonb,2,'100,000 - 40,000 = 60,000.','original'),
('127.251.d.16.G',4,'Return on investment in that example is:','["40%", "60%", "250%", "150%"]'::jsonb,3,'60,000 / 40,000 = 1.5.','original'),
('127.252.d.1.A',3,'The benefit of financial planning is that it helps you:','["Reach goals and prepare for emergencies", "Spend more", "Avoid saving", "Ignore bills"]'::jsonb,0,'Planning guides decisions.','original'),
('127.252.d.1.A',4,'A written financial plan should include goals, a budget, and:','["A guess", "A timeline for reaching goals", "No dates", "A secret"]'::jsonb,1,'Plans need timelines.','original'),
('127.252.d.1.B',3,'Paying bills on time and being honest on loan applications show:','["Carelessness", "Neglect", "Personal financial responsibility", "Dishonesty"]'::jsonb,2,'Responsibility builds trust.','original'),
('127.252.d.1.B',4,'Ethical financial behavior includes:','["Hiding income", "Skipping taxes", "Lying on applications", "Reporting income accurately on tax forms"]'::jsonb,3,'Honesty is ethical.','original'),
('127.252.d.2.A',3,'A student comparing college, military, and job options is:','["Understanding postsecondary opportunities", "Guessing", "Delaying", "Ignoring options"]'::jsonb,0,'Exploring options informs planning.','original'),
('127.252.d.2.A',4,'Which source gives information on careers and pay?','["A rumor", "The Bureau of Labor Statistics", "A song", "A comic"]'::jsonb,1,'BLS publishes career data.','original'),
('127.252.d.2.B',3,'A postsecondary plan should include application deadlines, costs, and:','["Rumors", "Mascots", "Funding sources", "Colors"]'::jsonb,2,'Plans need funding.','original'),
('127.252.d.2.B',4,'A student who lists deadlines for applications, FAFSA, and scholarships is:','["Skipping college", "Ignoring costs", "Avoiding aid", "Creating a postsecondary plan"]'::jsonb,3,'Planning organizes steps.','original'),
('127.252.d.3.A',3,'Cash, checks, debit cards, and credit cards are all:','["Forms of financial exchange", "Forms of taxes", "Types of loans only", "Types of insurance"]'::jsonb,0,'They transfer value.','original'),
('127.252.d.3.A',4,'Which form of payment borrows money from a lender?','["Money order", "Credit card", "Debit card", "Cash"]'::jsonb,1,'Credit cards extend credit.','original'),
('127.252.d.3.B',3,'Paper money and coins are examples of:','["Securities", "Credit", "Physical currency", "Digital currency only"]'::jsonb,2,'Physical forms.','original'),
('127.252.d.3.B',4,'Online-only payment systems like mobile wallets are examples of:','["Paper currency", "Barter", "Coins", "Electronic or digital currency forms"]'::jsonb,3,'They move value digitally.','original'),
('127.252.d.3.C',3,'When money is used to buy goods, it serves as a:','["Medium of exchange", "Store of value only", "Loan", "Tax"]'::jsonb,0,'Money enables trade.','original'),
('127.252.d.3.C',4,'Saving money for future use shows money as a:','["Unit of account", "Store of value", "Barter", "Medium of exchange"]'::jsonb,1,'Money holds value.','original'),
('127.252.d.3.D',3,'Income earned from owning rental property is:','["Wages", "Interest", "Rent income", "Tips"]'::jsonb,2,'Rent is a source.','original'),
('127.252.d.3.D',4,'Income that comes from stock dividends is a return on:','["Labor", "Loan", "Gift", "Investment"]'::jsonb,3,'Dividends reward owners.','original'),
('127.252.d.3.E',3,'Money today is worth more than the same amount in the future because it can:','["Earn interest", "Lose value always", "Be taxed", "Expire"]'::jsonb,0,'Time value of money.','original'),
('127.252.d.3.E',4,'$100 invested at 10% for one year grows to:','["$100", "$110", "$101", "$120"]'::jsonb,1,'100 x 1.10 = 110.','original'),
('127.252.d.3.F',3,'Credit allows people to:','["Pay no interest", "Skip bills", "Buy now and pay later", "Avoid payments"]'::jsonb,2,'Credit defers payment.','original'),
('127.252.d.3.F',4,'Good credit is important because it can lead to:','["Denied jobs", "Fewer options", "Higher rates", "Lower interest rates on loans"]'::jsonb,3,'Credit affects cost.','original'),
('127.252.d.4.A',3,'A business owned by one person is a:','["Sole proprietorship", "Corporation", "Partnership", "Cooperative"]'::jsonb,0,'One owner.','original'),
('127.252.d.4.A',4,'A business owned by shareholders and legally separate from its owners is a:','["Partnership", "Corporation", "Franchise only", "Sole proprietorship"]'::jsonb,1,'Corporations are separate legal entities.','original'),
('127.252.d.4.B',3,'Profit equals revenue minus:','["Assets", "Wages only", "Expenses", "Taxes only"]'::jsonb,2,'Profit is what remains.','original'),
('127.252.d.4.B',4,'A business has $90,000 in revenue and $70,000 in expenses. Profit is:','["$160,000", "$90,000", "$70,000", "$20,000"]'::jsonb,3,'90,000 - 70,000 = 20,000.','original')
