('127.252.d.5.A',3,'Taxes are collected to pay for:','["Individual hobbies", "Private jets", "Public services such as schools and roads", "Store profits"]'::jsonb,2,'Taxes fund government.','original'),
('127.252.d.5.A',4,'A tax based on a percentage of income is:','["A property tax", "A sales tax", "A tariff", "An income tax"]'::jsonb,3,'Income taxes are based on earnings.','original'),
('127.252.d.5.B',3,'Government changes to spending and taxes are:','["Fiscal policy", "Trade policy only", "Dress policy", "Monetary policy"]'::jsonb,0,'Fiscal policy is government budgeting.','original'),
('127.252.d.5.B',4,'Adjusting interest rates and the money supply is:','["Tax policy", "Monetary policy", "Budget policy", "Fiscal policy"]'::jsonb,1,'The Fed conducts it.','original'),
('127.252.d.5.C',3,'When more people want to borrow money, interest rates tend to:','["Fall", "Vanish", "Rise", "Stay at zero"]'::jsonb,2,'Demand raises the price of money.','original'),
('127.252.d.5.C',4,'When the supply of money in banks increases, interest rates tend to:','["Rise", "Double", "Stay fixed", "Fall"]'::jsonb,3,'More supply lowers price.','original'),
('127.252.d.5.D',3,'The Federal Reserve is the nation''s:','["Central bank", "Tax agency", "Court", "Largest retailer"]'::jsonb,0,'The Fed oversees monetary policy.','original'),
('127.252.d.5.D',4,'The Federal Reserve can raise interest rates to:','["Create jobs directly", "Slow inflation", "End taxes", "Increase inflation"]'::jsonb,1,'Higher rates cool spending.','original'),
('127.252.d.6.A',3,'Consumer spending is a major part of an economy because it:','["Has no effect", "Lowers growth always", "Drives demand for goods and services", "Raises unemployment"]'::jsonb,2,'Spending powers the economy.','original'),
('127.252.d.6.A',4,'A sharp decline in consumer spending often signals:','["Zero inflation", "No change", "A booming economy", "A weakening economy"]'::jsonb,3,'Falling demand is a warning.','original'),
('127.252.d.6.B',3,'High unemployment in a country usually means:','["Lower income and spending for many families", "More spending", "Less need for budgets", "Higher income for all"]'::jsonb,0,'Job loss reduces demand.','original'),
('127.252.d.6.B',4,'The unemployment rate measures the share of the labor force that is:','["Retired", "Actively seeking work but without a job", "In school", "Employed"]'::jsonb,1,'It excludes people not looking.','original'),
('127.252.d.6.C',3,'Gross Domestic Product measures:','["The number of jobs", "Tax revenue", "The total value of goods and services produced in a country", "Total savings"]'::jsonb,2,'GDP is output.','original'),
('127.252.d.6.C',4,'A rising GDP generally indicates:','["Collapse", "Deflation", "Recession", "Economic growth"]'::jsonb,3,'Growth means more output.','original'),
('127.252.d.6.D',3,'If prices rise 3% a year, a $100 item next year costs:','["$103", "$130", "$300", "$97"]'::jsonb,0,'100 x 1.03 = 103.','original'),
('127.252.d.6.D',4,'Inflation hurts people on fixed incomes because their money:','["Buys more", "Buys less over time", "Is taxed less", "Earns more"]'::jsonb,1,'Purchasing power falls.','original'),
('127.252.d.6.E',3,'The tradeoff between unemployment and inflation suggests that lower unemployment can sometimes bring:','["No change", "Lower inflation always", "Higher inflation", "Deflation"]'::jsonb,2,'Phillips curve tradeoff.','original'),
('127.252.d.6.E',4,'Policymakers trying to lower inflation may accept:','["Lower prices for sure", "No tradeoffs", "Higher wages", "Higher unemployment in the short run"]'::jsonb,3,'Tradeoffs exist.','original'),
('127.252.d.6.F',3,'When interest rates rise, borrowing costs for loans generally:','["Increase", "Decrease", "Vanish", "Stay the same"]'::jsonb,0,'Higher rates cost more.','original'),
('127.252.d.6.F',4,'Rising interest rates usually make saving accounts:','["Pay less", "Pay more interest", "Disappear", "Charge fees only"]'::jsonb,1,'Savers benefit.','original'),
('127.252.d.6.G',3,'During an economic expansion, personal financial decisions often include:','["Fewer jobs", "Higher unemployment", "More job opportunities and rising incomes", "Lower wages"]'::jsonb,2,'Expansion helps workers.','original'),
('127.252.d.6.G',4,'In a recession, a wise household might:','["Take on more debt", "Ignore budgets", "Spend freely", "Build emergency savings and cut nonessential spending"]'::jsonb,3,'Prepare for uncertainty.','original'),
('127.252.d.7.A',3,'A foreign war that raises oil prices can affect personal finances by:','["Raising gas and shipping costs", "Erasing debt", "Raising wages", "Lowering all prices"]'::jsonb,0,'Global events affect prices.','original'),
('127.252.d.7.A',4,'International events matter to personal finance because they can influence:','["Nothing", "Prices, jobs, and investment values", "Only governments", "Only foreign people"]'::jsonb,1,'The world economy is linked.','original'),
('127.252.d.7.B',3,'If $1 buys 17 pesos, how many dollars does 85 pesos buy?','["$17", "$50", "$5", "$1"]'::jsonb,2,'85 / 17 = 5.','original'),
('127.252.d.7.B',4,'When the dollar strengthens against other currencies, imports for U.S. buyers generally become:','["Banned", "Unavailable", "More expensive", "Cheaper"]'::jsonb,3,'A strong dollar buys more.','original'),
('127.252.d.7.C',3,'Moving factory jobs overseas is a labor issue that can affect:','["Local wages and employment", "Nothing", "Only weather", "Only taxes"]'::jsonb,0,'Labor markets are global.','original'),
('127.252.d.7.C',4,'Labor laws that differ between countries can influence:','["Weather", "Where companies choose to produce goods", "Only prices of paper", "Nothing"]'::jsonb,1,'Costs vary by country.','original'),
('127.252.d.8.A',3,'A personal budget should be based on:','["A friend''s budget", "Last year''s weather", "Income and personal financial goals", "Guesses"]'::jsonb,2,'Budgets reflect goals.','original'),
('127.252.d.8.A',4,'After listing income, the next step in budgeting is to:','["Delete goals", "Spend everything", "Ignore costs", "List fixed and variable expenses"]'::jsonb,3,'Expenses come next.','original'),
('127.252.d.8.B',3,'The benefit of saving and investing is that money can:','["Grow over time", "Lose all value", "Disappear", "Be taxed twice"]'::jsonb,0,'Growth builds wealth.','original'),
('127.252.d.8.B',4,'An emergency fund is useful because it:','["Earns no value", "Covers unexpected costs without debt", "Increases spending", "Replaces income"]'::jsonb,1,'Savings prevent borrowing.','original'),
('127.252.d.8.C',3,'Tax liability is the amount of tax:','["A person earns", "The bank owes", "A person owes", "The seller keeps"]'::jsonb,2,'Liability means owed.','original'),
('127.252.d.8.C',4,'A refund occurs when:','["No tax is withheld", "Income is zero", "Tax owed exceeds withholding", "Withholding exceeds the tax owed"]'::jsonb,3,'Over-withholding is refunded.','original'),
('127.252.d.8.D',3,'On a pay stub, net pay is the:','["Amount you take home after deductions", "Pay before deductions", "Total taxes", "Hours worked"]'::jsonb,0,'Net equals take-home.','original'),
('127.252.d.8.D',4,'Year-to-date totals on a pay stub show:','["Your credit score", "Cumulative earnings and deductions for the year so far", "Last month only", "Next year''s pay"]'::jsonb,1,'YTD tracks totals.','original'),
('127.252.d.8.E',4,'A check you wrote that has not yet cleared is called:','["Deposited", "Overdrawn", "Outstanding", "Canceled"]'::jsonb,2,'It is not yet on the statement.','original'),
('127.252.d.8.F',3,'Keeping receipts, pay stubs, and tax returns is:','["Hiding income", "Unneeded", "Wasting space", "Maintaining financial records"]'::jsonb,3,'Records support taxes and disputes.','original'),
('127.252.d.8.F',4,'Tax returns should generally be kept for at least:','["Three years", "One month", "One week", "One day"]'::jsonb,0,'The IRS can audit within about three years.','original'),
('127.252.d.8.G',3,'Evaluating a credit score means looking at the number and:','["Its font", "The factors that influence it", "Its date of printing only", "Its color"]'::jsonb,1,'Understand the drivers.','original'),
('127.252.d.8.G',4,'Which action is most likely to improve a credit score?','["Opening many accounts at once", "Maxing out cards", "Paying bills on time", "Missing payments"]'::jsonb,2,'On-time payments help.','original'),
('127.252.d.8.H',3,'Placing a fraud alert on your credit file is an example of:','["Budgeting", "Tax filing", "Bad credit", "Identity theft protection"]'::jsonb,3,'Alerts guard against misuse.','original'),
('127.252.d.8.H',4,'Monitoring your accounts regularly helps you:','["Spot unauthorized activity quickly", "Lose track", "Raise limits", "Lower scores"]'::jsonb,0,'Early detection limits damage.','original'),
('127.252.d.8.I',3,'A W-2 form reports:','["Loan balances", "Wages paid and taxes withheld", "Rent paid", "Credit score"]'::jsonb,1,'Employers issue W-2s.','original'),
('127.252.d.8.I',4,'To complete a simple tax return, a filer enters W-2 wages and then:','["Doubles income", "Ignores the W-2", "Subtracts the standard deduction to find taxable income", "Adds the deduction"]'::jsonb,2,'The standard deduction lowers taxable income.','original'),
('127.252.d.9.A',3,'Banks and credit unions are examples of:','["Auto dealers", "Retail stores", "Insurers only", "Financial service providers"]'::jsonb,3,'They offer accounts and loans.','original'),
('127.252.d.9.A',4,'Which provider is a member-owned cooperative?','["Credit union", "Pawn shop", "Stock broker", "Commercial bank"]'::jsonb,0,'Members own credit unions.','original'),
('127.252.d.9.B',3,'When choosing a bank, a consumer should compare:','["Logos", "Fees, interest rates, and convenience", "Building colors", "Slogans"]'::jsonb,1,'Cost and access matter.','original'),
('127.252.d.9.B',4,'A fee-free checking account with online access suits someone who wants:','["Branch-only service", "No access", "Low cost and easy management", "High fees"]'::jsonb,2,'Matches needs.','original'),
('127.252.d.9.C',3,'Most people finance a home or car with a:','["Coupon", "Gift", "Tax credit", "Loan"]'::jsonb,3,'Loans fund large purchases.','original'),
('127.252.d.9.C',4,'Long-term assets like a house are usually financed over:','["Many years", "One week", "One day", "One month"]'::jsonb,0,'Long terms lower payments.','original'),
('127.252.d.9.D',3,'Lenders evaluate a loan applicant using income, debts, credit history, and:','["Favorite team", "Collateral", "Hobbies", "Height"]'::jsonb,1,'Collateral secures loans.','original'),
('127.252.d.9.D',4,'The ratio of monthly debt payments to monthly income is the:','["Interest rate", "Down payment", "Debt-to-income ratio", "Credit limit"]'::jsonb,2,'DTI is a key measure.','original')
